There's a bug in the logic for handling duplicate slugs that can result in duplicate `id` attributes on the page. This happens if a heading's text content naturally produces a slug that has already been generated for a previous duplicate heading (e.g., headings "My Title", "My Title", "My Title-2"). This will cause anchor links to behave unpredictably.

A more robust approach is to check if an ID already exists in the DOM before assigning it.

Additionally, this new block of code uses `var` and `function` declarations, which is inconsistent with the surrounding code in this file that uses modern ES6+ features like `const`, `let`, and arrow functions. I recommend updating the entire IIFE (lines 146-202) to use modern syntax for consistency and maintainability.

Here is a suggested replacement for the ID generation logic that fixes the bug and uses modern syntax:

```javascript
    document.querySelectorAll('.docs-content h1, .docs-content h2, .docs-content h3, .docs-content h4').forEach(heading => {
        if (heading.id) return;
        const baseSlug = slugify(heading.textContent);
        if (!baseSlug) return;

        let slug = baseSlug;
        let counter = 1;
        while (document.getElementById(slug)) {
            slug = `${baseSlug}-${counter++}`;
        }
        heading.id = slug;
    });
```

