### Race Condition in Asynchronous File Loading

A race condition exists in `renderFile` because it is an asynchronous function that fetches file content over the network. If a user clicks multiple files in rapid succession, multiple fetch requests will run concurrently. Since network responses can resolve out of order, a slower response from an earlier clicked file could overwrite the content of a faster response from a later clicked file, leading to the wrong file content being displayed.

### Solution

Before updating the UI with `setContent` after any asynchronous operation (`await`), verify that the file being rendered (`path`) is still the currently selected file (`selectedAgentsPath`). If it is not, discard the response and return early.

```javascript
    async function renderFile(path) {
        if (filePreviews[path]) {
            setContent(path, filePreviews[path], githubLinkForPath(path, 'blob'));
            return;
        }
        setContent(path, 'Loading file...', githubLinkForPath(path, 'blob'));
        try {
            const response = await fetch(`${RAW_BASE}${path}`);
            if (!response.ok) throw new Error(`raw fetch ${response.status}`);
            const text = await response.text();
            if (selectedAgentsPath !== path) return;
            setContent(path, text.slice(0, 60000), githubLinkForPath(path, 'blob'));
        } catch (error) {
            if (selectedAgentsPath !== path) return;
            setContent(path, 'Could not load this file from GitHub raw content.', githubLinkForPath(path, 'blob'));
        }
    }
```

